Generator: Model-driven synthetic Latendresse profile generator, with empirical structure extracted from the bundled canonical biological model.
Application: Synthetic linear-real and floating-point benchmarks modeled on the structure of the existing SMT-LIB QF_LRA Latendresse benchmarks [1], which originate in flux-balance-analysis and pathway-database model-completion applications [2].
Biological inspiration: E. coli growth on glycerol with discrete objective, nutrient, and secretion choices, specifically the ecoliMILPglycerolYices3 model in the existing SMT-LIB Latendresse family.
Target solver: cvc5
Time limit: 300

The model has 20 real-valued reaction variables, 3 real-valued 0/1 selectors (1 objective, 1 nutrient, and 1 secretion), and 12 exact mass-balance rows spanning 1 to 9 reactions. Every selector has a paired reaction with the two inequalities used by the canonical glycerol model. The objective preserves the canonical +10000 selector rewards, nutrient/secretion costs, -1/10 background flux cost, and objective-reaction coefficient ratios under one positive size-dependent normalization.

Objective regime: boundary. The mixed glycerol objective is lower-bounded at the independently attainable maximum, making the QF_LRA instance satisfiable.
Numeric profile: native. Flux bounds use 1000, and row coefficients are sampled from the canonical family model.

[1] Timothy King, Clark Barrett, and Bruno Dutertre, Simplex with Sum of Infeasibilities for SMT, FMCAD 2013.
[2] Mario Latendresse, Markus Krummenacker, Miles Trupp, and Peter D. Karp, Construction and completion of flux balance models from pathway databases, Bioinformatics 28(3), 388-396, 2012.
|)
